%S A399308 70,140,280,560,345,690,1380,2760,1705,1055,2110,1305,2610,5220,3225,
%T A399308 6450,3985,7970,4925,3045,6090,3765,7530,4655,9310,5755,11510,7115,
%U A399308 14230,8795,5435,3360,6720,13440,8305,16610,10265,6345,12690,25380,15685,9695,19390,11985
%N A399308 The periodic cycle of 70 in the "Lucas-Collatz" function: a(0)=70; for n>0, if a(n-1) is Lucas-odd (A399306), then a(n)=2*a(n-1); if a(n-1) is Lucas-even (A399305), then a(n) is the integer whose minimal Lucas representation (A130310) results from removing the final 0 of A130310(a(n-1)).
%C A399308 This periodic cycle comprises 122 integers. It includes 5 integers > 1000000 (one million), the largest of which is a(74) = 1863240.
%C A399308 Other small integers which do not occur within this cycle itself, but whose orbits reach this cycle, include 35, 45, 55, 60, 75, 90, 95, 100, 120, 125. The integer 115 = a(121) occurs as the last term in the cycle before it returns to a(122) = 70.
%C A399308 It is interesting that the Lucas representation of 70 = 47+18+4+1, which is similar to that of 23 = 18+4+1, the smallest value of the other known long periodic cycle of this function (A399307).
%C A399308 It follows from the definition of the function and the properties of Lucas numbers that the orbit of any multiple of 5 contains only multiples of 5.
%C A399308 Conjecture: The orbit of every positive multiple of 5 reaches either this cycle of 70 or the cycle 5, 10, 5.
%H A399308 Geoffrey Caveney, <a href="/A399308/b399308.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..122</a>
%e A399308 a(1) = 140 = 2*a(0) since a(0) = 70 is Lucas-odd because removing the final 0 of A130310(70) = 101001010 does not result in a valid minimal Lucas representation of another integer.
%e A399308 a(4) = 345 since a(3) = 560 is Lucas-even and removing the final 0 of A130310(560) = 10000010010100 results in A130310(345) = 1000001001010.
%Y A399308 Cf. A399305, A399306, A399307, A130310, A000032, A008884, A003124.
